IMPORTANT — THE APACHE-2.0 GRANT COVERS THIS REPOSITORY'S CODE ONLY
================================================================================

The code in this repository is Apache-2.0. The machine-learning model it is
built to invoke is NOT, and this repository grants you no rights to it.

TRIBE v2 (Meta FAIR / facebookresearch) — the brain-encoding model this
pipeline scores video with — is released under CC-BY-NC-4.0
(Attribution–NonCommercial 4.0 International). That license explicitly
prohibits use "primarily intended for or directed toward commercial advantage
or monetary compensation."

What this means in practice:

  * Running this code against TRIBE v2 weights for research, education,
    evaluation, or personal use is fine.

  * Running this code against TRIBE v2 weights as part of a paid product,
    a client deliverable, or any other commercial offering is NOT covered by
    Apache-2.0 and is very likely a violation of Meta's model license.
    Apache-2.0 on this repository does not and cannot relicense TRIBE v2.

  * "Sell the service, not the model" does not resolve this. If the paid
    deliverable depends on running the NonCommercial model, it is
    commercial use of that model.

A commercially clean path exists but requires different weights — training an
architecture with a permissive code license (e.g. VIBE, MIT) from its own
initialization on CC0 data (e.g. CNeuroMod). Weights derived from, initialized
from, distilled from, or fine-tuned on CC-BY-NC weights inherit the
NonCommercial restriction.

Note also that non-commercial *training data* can constrain commercial use of
an otherwise-permissive model. NSD is CC-BY-NC-ND; HCP's open-access terms are
ambiguous on redistribution of derived weights; CNeuroMod is CC0 and is the
cleanest available foundation. This is a summary, not legal advice — get
counsel before commercializing anything trained on restricted data.


